#!/usr/bin/env bash
#
# pre-push -- gate a push on this repository's own CI, DERIVED from
# `.github/workflows` via `scripts/local_ci.py`, instead of a second list of
# commands kept by hand.
#
# =============================================================================
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#
# A hand-written list of "the commands CI runs" is a list that lies the day a
# workflow file changes and nobody updates the copy. This hook does not carry
# that list: it calls `scripts/local_ci.py`, which reads
# `.github/workflows/*.yml` itself and derives which `run:` steps are code,
# which need the GitHub runner, and which build the environment (see that
# file's own docstring for the classification). This hook only reads the exit
# code `scripts/local_ci.py` returns and turns it into a push decision.
#
# =============================================================================
# THREE VERDICTS THAT MUST NEVER ADD UP TO ONE "VERMELHO"
#
#   1. TESTE VERMELHO -- a code step that `local_ci.py` ran (ruff, pyright,
#      pytest, `scripts/verify.py`, ...) exited non-zero on its own logic.
#      This is the only case that should ever be read as "the change is bad".
#
#   2. FALHA DE MAQUINA -- the `local_ci.py` process itself was killed by a
#      signal (OOM killer, `kill -9`, a crash) rather than exiting on its own.
#      In POSIX shells, an exit status above 128 is always `128 + signal
#      number`, never a value the program chose -- so `$STATUS -gt 128` is
#      read as machine death, independent of what any step inside was doing.
#      This says nothing about the code; the run did not finish measuring it.
#
#   3. FALHA DE AMBIENTE -- something the CI needs is missing HERE: `uv` or
#      `bash` not on `PATH` (checked below before anything runs), or exit 127
#      -- the shell's own convention for "command not found" -- surfacing
#      from inside the run. `local_ci.py` also exits `2` when it cannot even
#      classify the workflow's steps (e.g. `.github/workflows` missing). None
#      of this says anything about the code either.
#
# Folding these three into one "push blocked" would refuse a push for a
# reason the author cannot fix by editing code, and would train everyone to
# reach for `--no-verify` the moment any of the three fires -- which defeats
# case 1, the one this hook exists for.
#
# =============================================================================
# RULESET RECONCILIATION RUNS HERE, NOT IN CI -- WHY
#
# `scripts/verify_ruleset.py` (`engineering_playbook.core.check_ruleset_reconciliation`)
# compares `.github/rulesets/main.yml` against the ruleset GitHub actually has
# applied on the server, via `gh api repos/:owner/:repo/rulesets`. That
# endpoint needs `administration` read on whichever token calls it, and
# GitHub refuses to even start a workflow that lists `administration` under a
# job's `permissions:` for `GITHUB_TOKEN` -- measured directly: the workflow
# was rejected at parse time, before any step ran, not merely that one step.
# There is no CI job this can run in with this repository's own token. It
# runs here instead, using the `gh` authentication (`gh auth login`/`GH_TOKEN`)
# the developer pushing already has on their own machine.
#
# TWO DIFFERENT ANSWERS, NEVER FOLDED INTO ONE
#
#   "Nao consegui medir" (no network, `gh` missing or unauthenticated, or
#   `verify_ruleset.py` failing before it reaches a comparison) is NOT the
#   same fact as "medi e diverge" (`gh` answered, the comparison ran, and the
#   file disagrees with the server). NFR-002 (spec 003) is explicit: "Controle
#   incapaz de obter a medicao retorna falha. Indisponibilidade de ambiente
#   nao equivale a conformidade." So BOTH cases refuse the push by default --
#   an owner decision, taken after weighing and rejecting the alternative
#   (warn-and-pass on "could not measure"), because that alternative reads a
#   push as reconciled when nobody actually compared anything, which is
#   exactly what NFR-002 forbids. The two cases still print different
#   messages (see below), because a developer who cannot fix a network
#   outage by editing code still needs to know which one hit them, and the
#   escape valve below only exists for the first.
#
#   The one relief this hook grants offline work is deliberate and visible:
#   `PLAYBOOK_ALLOW_UNMEASURED_RULESET=1` lets a push through when the
#   measurement itself could not be taken, and only then -- "medi e diverge"
#   has no escape valve at all, ever. Using it costs exactly what it looks
#   like it costs: the push happens without knowing whether the applied
#   ruleset still matches `.github/rulesets/main.yml`, and the hook prints
#   that fact loudly rather than letting the push look clean.
#
# =============================================================================
# THE KNOWN WAYS PAST THIS GUARD, WRITTEN ON PURPOSE
#
# A guard whose weakness is unwritten is theatre, not a guard. So:
#
#   1. `git push --no-verify` skips this file entirely. Nothing here can turn
#      that off; a push made this way was never measured by this hook.
#   2. `git config core.hooksPath <somewhere-else>` points git at another
#      directory and nothing in this one runs at all. This file cannot detect
#      that it was bypassed this way.
#   3. The ruleset check only measures anything on a machine that has `gh`
#      installed and authenticated already. This protects whoever pushes
#      from such a machine; a push from anywhere else (a machine without
#      `gh`, a CI job with no human's `gh auth`, a raw `git push` to a mirror)
#      is not measured by this piece of the hook at all -- and per NFR-002,
#      not being measured means the push is REFUSED, not waved through.
#   4. `PLAYBOOK_ALLOW_UNMEASURED_RULESET=1` is the one deliberate escape
#      valve, and it only opens the door NFR-002 closed for "could not
#      measure" -- it does nothing for "measured and diverges". Setting it
#      makes the hook print, every time, that the ruleset was not checked and
#      the push happened anyway; it never lets that fact pass in silence. The
#      cost of using it: the pusher does not know whether the applied ruleset
#      still matches `.github/rulesets/main.yml`.
#   5. This compares the versioned file against the server; it does not stop
#      anyone with admin rights from changing the server ruleset directly.
#      `.github/rulesets/main.yml` says so itself: "Declarative proposal
#      only. Do not apply remotely without explicit authorization." The
#      server does not enforce what this file declares unless someone with
#      that access applies it, and this hook cannot make that happen.
#   6. If `scripts/verify_ruleset.py` does not exist in this checkout (a
#      derived project that never adopted this control), the ruleset check is
#      skipped entirely rather than failing the hook -- see below.
#
# What this guard does NOT do: it does not run `--affected` (that mode is
# documented in `local_ci.py` as narrower than the merge gate on purpose), it
# does not push anything itself, and it does not judge branch names or commit
# messages -- see `pre-commit` for the one check this repository makes at
# commit time.
# =============================================================================

set -uo pipefail

REPO_ROOT="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null || echo "")"
if [ -z "$REPO_ROOT" ]; then
    echo "pre-push: nao consegui resolver a raiz do repositorio via git; abortando." >&2
    exit 1
fi
cd "$REPO_ROOT" || exit 1

fail_environment() {
    cat >&2 <<EOF

  BLOQUEADO POR AMBIENTE, NAO POR CODIGO: $1

  Isto nao mede nada sobre o que voce mudou. Resolva o ambiente e rode de
  novo. 'git push --no-verify' pula este hook inteiro, se voce souber o que
  esta fazendo -- mas isso significa que este push nao foi medido por ele.

EOF
    exit 1
}

command -v uv >/dev/null 2>&1 || fail_environment "'uv' nao foi encontrado no PATH"
command -v bash >/dev/null 2>&1 || fail_environment "'bash' nao foi encontrado no PATH (os workflows sao bash)"

echo "pre-push: derivando o CI de .github/workflows via scripts/local_ci.py ..." >&2

uv run python scripts/local_ci.py
STATUS=$?

if [ "$STATUS" -gt 128 ]; then
    SIGNAL=$((STATUS - 128))
    cat >&2 <<EOF

  BLOQUEADO POR FALHA DE MAQUINA, NAO POR CODIGO: o processo do CI local
  morreu pelo sinal $SIGNAL (exit $STATUS = 128 + sinal), nao pela logica de
  nenhum passo. A corrida nao terminou de medir o que voce mudou.

  Rode 'uv run python scripts/local_ci.py' de novo, sozinho, para ver se o
  sinal se repete (memoria, processo morto por fora, etc.) antes de tentar o
  push outra vez.

EOF
    exit 1
fi

if [ "$STATUS" -eq 127 ]; then
    fail_environment "um comando dentro do CI local nao foi encontrado (exit 127)"
fi

if [ "$STATUS" -eq 2 ]; then
    fail_environment "scripts/local_ci.py nao conseguiu classificar os passos do workflow (exit 2) -- veja a saida acima"
fi

if [ "$STATUS" -ne 0 ]; then
    cat >&2 <<EOF

  PUSH RECUSADO: o CI local, derivado de .github/workflows, rodou e um passo
  de CODIGO falhou (exit $STATUS). Veja a saida acima para o passo e o motivo.

  Isto NAO e o portao de merge -- e a mesma verificacao rodando aqui antes de
  gastar um minuto do Actions. Corrija e rode de novo.

EOF
    exit 1
fi

RULESET_SCRIPT="$REPO_ROOT/scripts/verify_ruleset.py"

if [ -f "$RULESET_SCRIPT" ]; then
    echo "pre-push: comparando .github/rulesets/main.yml com o ruleset aplicado no servidor ..." >&2

    RULESET_OUTPUT="$(uv run python scripts/verify_ruleset.py 2>&1)"
    RULESET_STATUS=$?

    echo "$RULESET_OUTPUT" >&2

    if [ "$RULESET_STATUS" -eq 0 ]; then
        : # medido e reconciliado -- nada a fazer
    elif printf '%s\n' "$RULESET_OUTPUT" | grep -q "gh nao respondeu"; then
        if [ "${PLAYBOOK_ALLOW_UNMEASURED_RULESET:-}" = "1" ]; then
            cat >&2 <<EOF

  AVISO: RULESET NAO MEDIDO -- PUSH SEGUINDO SO PORQUE
  PLAYBOOK_ALLOW_UNMEASURED_RULESET=1 ESTA DEFINIDA.

  'gh' nao respondeu (rede ausente, 'gh auth login' pendente, ou 'gh' fora do
  PATH) -- veja a saida acima. Este push NAO sabe se o ruleset aplicado no
  servidor ainda bate com '.github/rulesets/main.yml'; a valvula so existe
  para destravar quem esta genuinamente sem como medir, nunca em silencio.
  Desligue a variavel assim que 'gh' voltar a responder.

EOF
        else
            cat >&2 <<EOF

  PUSH RECUSADO: NAO CONSEGUI MEDIR O RULESET DO SERVIDOR.

  'gh' nao respondeu (rede ausente, 'gh auth login' pendente, ou 'gh' fora do
  PATH) -- veja a saida acima. NFR-002 (spec 003): um controle incapaz de
  obter a medicao retorna falha, indisponibilidade de ambiente nao equivale a
  conformidade -- este push nao foi medido e por isso nao passa.

  Resolva 'gh' (rede, 'gh auth login') e rode de novo. Se estiver
  genuinamente sem como medir agora e precisar empurrar mesmo assim, defina
  PLAYBOOK_ALLOW_UNMEASURED_RULESET=1 -- o hook vai avisar, em voz alta, que
  o push saiu sem essa medicao.

EOF
            exit 1
        fi
    elif printf '%s\n' "$RULESET_OUTPUT" | grep -q "^ERROR:"; then
        cat >&2 <<EOF

  PUSH RECUSADO: o ruleset aplicado no servidor DIVERGE de
  '.github/rulesets/main.yml' (exit $RULESET_STATUS). Veja a saida acima.

  Isto foi MEDIDO, nao e falha de rede: 'gh' respondeu e a comparacao rodou.
  Reconcilie o arquivo com o que esta aplicado no servidor, ou peca a quem
  tem acesso administrativo para aplicar o arquivo, antes de empurrar de
  novo.

EOF
        exit 1
    else
        fail_environment "scripts/verify_ruleset.py falhou de um jeito inesperado (exit $RULESET_STATUS) -- nem confirmou medicao nem apontou divergencia; veja a saida acima"
    fi
fi

exit 0
